## Chiral transfer in coordination complexes: towards molecular materialsw Jeanne Crassous\*
## 1. Introduction
A molecule devoid of improper symmetry axis is chiral and exists as two mirror-images that are non-superimposable and commonly called enantiomers.1 Chirality is a signature of life, of biological molecules, and of various inert objects (corkscrew,. . .). In chemistry, it is expressed not only at the molecular level but also at the supramolecular level and in materials. Chirality in materials involve the dissymmetric arrangement of molecules in a noncovalent assembly (enantiomorphism). Chirality can also be expressed in terms of symmetry elements: a molecule is chiral if it belongs to the $C_{n}$ or $D_{n},$ I or $T$ point groups. There exist numerous ways of generating chiral coordination complexes and assemblies.2,3
The chirality may arise from the spatial arrangement of chelating ligands around a metal ion, from the formation of double or triple helices, from the helical twist of a macrocyclic ligand, etc. These chiral compounds may be separated into enantiopure forms, for example by spontaneous resolution4 or by other resolution methods such as HPLC over chiral stationary phases.5 In more convenient cases, the enantiopure metal complexes and their supramolecular assemblies may be obtained by diastereoselective synthesis from nonracemic chiral organic building blocks used as chiral ligands. These ligands may determine the configuration of the overall complex. Generally, this strategy is successful when one of the possible diastereomers is thermodynamically favoured. This interesting case, where a ligand transfers its chirality to the metal centre, has been called ‘‘predetermined chirality’’.6–9 Propagation of the chiral information through specific interactions and organization in materials or supramolecular assemblies constitutes another important case of chiral transfer in coordination complexes.
Controlling the chirality in coordination complexes and in materials is also a way of relating their properties and reactivity to their structure and composition, in a rational and predictive way. It allows the design of metal complexes based materials with controlled topology and with specific physical properties such as chiroptical properties (circular dichroism (CD), circularly polarised luminescence (CPL), optical rotation), chiroptical switching processes, nonlinear optical (NLO) activity, magneto-chiral effects, allosteric effects, etc. Chiral metal organic frameworks (MOF), polyoxometallates (POM) or metallomesogens are emerging materials with promising properties. Establishing rules predicting the stereochemistry at the metal centre from ligands is not the purpose of this review. This task is up to now almost impossible.6 Throughout this critical review, illustrative examples highlight the essential aspects of chiral transfer in coordination complexes with the final purpose of having a molecular material bearing a specific property. Special attention will be paid on the chiroptical properties. The problems of chiral transfer in systems involving surfaces and in nanoparticles are not discussed here because they go beyond the general concepts of coordination chemistry. Similarly, asymmetric catalysis is beyond the scope of this review.
## 2. Chiral transfer in molecular complexes
## 2.1 General aspects
Due to the multitude of metallic ions and their different coordination numbers and geometries, the stereochemistry of coordination compounds appears very rich.2,10 Contrary to organic chemistry where the different methods to access enantiopure compounds are well-developed, general strategies giving enantiopure coordination complexes are still difficult to establish. Certainly the most convenient way is the use of ‘‘chiral transfer’’. Indeed, according to Knof and von Zelewsky, ‘‘The use of organic ligands, where the configuration can be controlled by known methods, is the main feature by which chiral information can be transferred to the metal centre. . . In this way, a predetermination of the absolute configuration at the metal centre can be reached’’. Because the lability of ligands in coordination complexes is well-known, multidentate chelating ligands are more efficient in chiral transfer. These ones will be either enantiopure, whose defined stereochemistry will be transferred to the metal centre in a controlled manner, or conformationaly labile. In the latter case, the ligand will be rigidified upon coordination into a chiral conformation and will give rise to an overall chiral complex (see the case of helicates in paragraph 3.1). There are in fact many factors that influence the stereochemistry at the metal centre: temperature, pressure, pH, concentrations, kinetics vs. thermodynamics, solvent effect, the solid state, steric hindrance, rigidity of the ligand, etc. The cycles (their size and the configuration they adopt) that are formed during the complexation process have also an influence on the final stereochemistry. Moreover, it is often observed that slightly modifying the chiral ligand may have significant influence on the stereochemistry at the metal. Being predictive is therefore very difficult. Each system must be considered as an individual one, and treated with precautions and with all the tools of stereochemistry in hand. A few examples of chiral transfer in coordination complexes displaying octahedral (OC-6), tetrahedral (T-4), distorted square planar (SP-4), and helical geometries are presented, together with chiral lanthanide complexes.
## 2.2 Geometrical considerations
2.2.1 Octahedral complexes. Octahedral complexes of ruthenium, osmium, iridium decorated with bipyridine (bpy) or phenylpyridine (ppy) ligands are known to display very interesting light-emitting properties. Octahedral $[\mathbf{M}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}]^{n+}$ complexes exist as two $\Delta$ and $\Lambda$ enantiomers while $[\mathrm{M}(\mathrm{ppy})_{3}]^{n+}$ complexes may additionally exist as fac or mer isomers because of the dissymmetric character of the ligand $C_{\mathrm{s}}$ symmetry).2 To circumvent this fac/mer isomerism, complexes possessing cage ligand structures have been developed. When such ligands are substituted with chiral arms, chiral transfer may occur, preventing the classical photoracemization of $\Delta/\Lambda$ species. For example, von Zelewsky and co-workers have developed $C_{3}$ -symmetric chiral ligands ()-L1 with terpene derivatives (Chiragen ligands) whose stereoselective coordination to $\mathrm{Ir}^{\mathrm{III}}$ gave rise to highly luminescent chiral tris-cyclometalated complexes fac-L-IrL1 (Fig. 1).11,12 More recently, a $C_{3}$ -symmetrical ligand based on a mesityl central moiety proved to selectively complex $\mathrm{{Ru}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ (see Fig. 1) with efficient chiral transfer, as evidenced by CD and CPL spectroscopy.13 While the $\mathrm{{Ru}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ complex appeared fluorescent, a strong phosphorescence was observed in the case of the $Z\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ complex. This probably came from a ligand centred triplet $(^{3}\mathrm{LC})$ state which was enhanced due to the rigidification of the $C_{3}$ -symmetrical chiral cage ligand as compared to its parent achiral ligand. Such highly phosphorescent complex may be of great interest in the field of triplet emitting OLEDs. Other types of chiral tripodal scaffolds that orient the three functional groups around the $C_{3}$ axis in a fac manner and in a preferential $\Delta$ or $\Lambda$ configuration have been prepared.14,15 An interesting example shows memory of chirality after cleavage of the three functional groups, giving rise to one pair of pure enantiomers.16 Other chiral complexes bearing cage ligands will be presented in section 2.3.1 in the case of lanthanides.
Enantiopure octahedral complexes display huge Cotton effect as a result of the exciton coupling of the three chromophoric ligands.17 CD spectroscopy is therefore a powerful tool to assess the chiral transfer from the ligand to the metal during the complexation process.18 However, it is not the case in chiral complexes displaying distorted square planar geometry around the metal for which the CD spectra are usually weak.19 The chiral transfer is sometimes better evidenced by X-ray crystallography.20,21
Optical activity can be induced in labile molecules or ions by the addition of a chiral species such as a chiral solvent, a chiral ion-pairing or a hydrogen-bonding agent. This phenomenon (displacement of a racemate in favour of one enantiomer) is called the Pfeiffer effect.22,23 In this context, TRISPHAT (tris(tetrachlorobenzenediolato)phosphate(V)) anion 1 has revealed itself as efficient in the diastereoselective ion-pairing with configurationally labile tris( $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dimethyl- $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine)- iron(II) $[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{bpy})_{3}]^{2+}\ 2$ (Fig. 2) and analogous complexes. The homochiral $^{(\Delta,\Delta)}$ or $(\Lambda,\Lambda)$ packing was favoured over the heterochiral one.24–27
2.2.2 Tetrahedral complexes. Pseudo-tetrahedral $\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{I}}$ complexes with two dissymmetric ligands of the type $\mathrm{[Cu(LL^{\prime})_{2}]}^{+}$ display spiro-like chirality and are generally labile.28 Some trigger must be found to stabilize the configuration at the metal centre. In some cases, a chiral counteranion such as TRISPHAT 1 can induce diastereoselectivity via stereoselective interactions between the ions, under a supramolecular stereocontrol as seen in the previous paragraph.22,24 Chiral $\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{I}}$ complexes can also be diastereoselectively formed from chiral unsymmetrical bidentate ligands by using steric hindrance as for pinene derivatives L3 (Fig. 3).29 They can also be incorporated into a topologically chiral structure such as a trefoil knot30 (see the dicopper trefoil knot $\mathrm{[Cu}_{2}({\bf L}4)]^{2+}$ in Fig. 3). Because trefoil knots require breaking and reforming of a covalent bond to racemize, these topologically chiral molecules are configurationally stable under non-destructive conditions. In addition, they display huge chiroptical properties31–33 and are valuable switching systems.34 Nitschke and co-workers have studied the chiral transfer in tetrahedral complexes formed from ligands subcomponents displaying central chirality.35,36 They recently observed solvent-tunable inversion of chirality, as depicted in Scheme 1. In complexes $^{3\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b}}$ , the intra-ligand hydrogen-bonding plays a crucial role in determining the helicity at the metal centre, as solute–solvent interactions have significant effect on the intramolecular interaction.
2.2.3 Distorted square-planar complexes. Bidentate ligands such as phenylpyridine, thiophene-pyridine or bipyridine derivatives may deviate from planarity under a complexation process, as in the well-known case of homoleptic biscyclometalated $\mathrm{Pt}^{\mathrm{II}}$ complexes 4 depicted in Scheme 2. In this helically distorted SP-4 complex, the two ligands display a syn configuration (trans effect). Furthermore, due to their strong steric hindrance, they are forced to overlap, giving rise to a chiral $C_{2}$ -symmetric complex having two possible $\Delta$ or $\Lambda$ configurations. By using chiral bipyridine derivatives of the Chiragen family such as L5, enantiopure complexes $\mathbf{\Delta\Delta\Delta\Delta\Delta\Delta\Delta\Delta\Delta\Delta}\Delta\mathbf{\Delta\mathrm{g}}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{5}]^{+}$ (see Scheme 2) have been prepared. $^{37-39}\mathrm{~A~}$ similar stereoselective process has been achieved in our group. Indeed, the coordination of aza[4]helicene-phospholes L6 to distorted square-planar $\mathrm{Pd}^{\mathrm{II}}$ metal centres (see Scheme 2) gave rise to new chiral complexes where the helices overlap and wind in the same helical sense.40
2.2.4 Helical complexes and metallofoldamers. Helical chirality or helicity of a molecule is generated by its secondary structure,41,42 and is expressed as two $P$ and $M$ forms (respectively, right- and left-handed). In this context, metallofoldamers are unnatural structures that fold into compact secondary structures upon binding to one or several metal centres.42 The secondary structure may be stabilized by specific interactions such as hydrogen bonding, steric repulsion, $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking, etc. There exist two general methods to prepare metallofoldamers as depicted in Scheme 3. In the most common one (case a), the metal coordination sphere is inherently chiral (the chirality is inherent to the geometry around the metal) whereas in case b, the metal coordination nucleates the formation of a non-biological single-stranded helix. In the latter method, the metal coordination sphere is not inherently chiral but it causes a series of cooperative non-covalent interactions that ultimately result in a folded structure.
Indeed, helicity can be induced upon coordination of open chain ligands to metal centres, as in the case of bilinone, an acyclic porphyrin derivative, which upon coordination to $Z\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ or other metallic ions44,45 gives single-stranded helical complexes (Scheme 4). Chiral functional groups have been introduced at the periphery of the bilinone ligand in order to bias the equilibrium between the $M$ and $P$ helices 5a and 5b. A diastereoselectivity occurred as observed by CD spectroscopy with large Cotton effects due to the helical conformation.46 In such complexes, the solvent shows significant influence on the $P/M$ equilibrium. Indeed, solvents with lower polarisability are more efficient in inducing helicity because they favour intramolecular van der Waals contacts responsible for diastereomeric discrimination.47 Although $Z\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ bilinone derivatives can be considered as foldamers, they cannot be extended to foldamers with longer chains (1D polymers).42 However, interesting allosteric effects have been observed in bilinone dimers for which the chiral recognition of amino-acids such as L-LeuOMe by one bilinone moiety triggers a conformational switch from $M,P$ heterochirality to $M,M$ homochirality in the second moiety and induces chiral amplification.48
Fox and co-workers have recently described metallofoldamers of type b (see Scheme 3) formed by the complexation of salen and salophen derivatives (Scheme 5).43,49,50 Complexes 6a and 6b are fluxional molecules in solution, and the thermodynamically most favoured conformations are helical and undergo $P/M$ interconversion. In order to bias the equilibrium towards the $P$ or the $M$ helix, two types of derivatives with central chirality have been tested. When chiral end groups with new favourable intramolecular H-bonds and steric interaction were placed at the periphery, such as in ligand L7, a strong bias towards one of the helicities was observed.49,50 On the other hand, when $(R,R)$ - or (S,S)-cyclohexanediamine moieties was used in place of the central achiral 1,2-diaminophenylene moiety, a $1:1$ equilibrium between the $M$ and $P$ helices was obtained. Thus, the chiral end-groups were strong directors of absolute helicity in salophen metallofoldamers, whereas central chiral cyclohexane diamine revealed only a weak chiral influence. Interestingly, metallofoldamer 6a could be reversibly switched by electrochemistry from the thermodynamically stable helical state (tetrahedral $\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}.$ ) to a nonhelical one (pentacoordinated ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{I\setminus}}$ ).43
An illustrative case of the control of kinetics vs. thermodynamics leading to different diastereomeric complexes has been described recently by Gregolinski and Lisowski. They reported a rare example of helicity inversion between the kinetic and thermodynamic complexation products of a macrocyclic ligand, as well as the isolation of both products in enantiopure forms.51 Indeed, they have shown that a solution of the kinetic complex $M\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{Yb}{\bf L}_{R R R R R})^{3+}$ 7a can be converted, upon heating, to the thermodynamic complex $P\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{YbL}_{R R R R R})^{3+}7$ b, with LRRRRRR being a nona-aza macrocycle with six stereogenic carbons (Scheme 6). These two complexes could be isolated separately and studied by X-ray crystallography and CD spectroscopy. The inversion of helicity was accompanied by an inversion of the CD properties, arising essentially from the chiral environment around the lanthanide ion. This example is different from the common cases, either where two diastereomeric complexes have analogous stabilities, or where one diastereomer is much more favored. Note that the helicity inversion is dependent on the nature of the lanthanide ion $\mathrm{Ln}^{\mathrm{III}}$ .
## 2.3 Selected chiroptical properties
2.3.1 Chiral luminescent lanthanide complexes. Among the most fascinating and useful chiral coordination compounds are the lanthanide complexes that can be used as probes for the characterization of chiral environments52–54 or as chiral luminescent complexes.55–61 Indeed, lanthanides (especially $\mathrm{Eu}^{\mathrm{III}}$ and $\mathrm{Tb}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ) are ideal candidates for the development of chiral CPL probes because they display large Stokes shifts, long lifetimes, narrow emission bands. CPL is the emission analogue of CD and is characterized by the $g$ factor. $^{62-65}{\mathrm{CPL}}$ has great potential to investigate the configurational as well as conformational changes in biological systems in solution because it combines the general sensitivity of luminescence measurements and the high specificity of the signal for the chiral environment. For instance, the conformational changes in calcium and iron binding proteins have been investigated using $\mathrm{Tb}^{\mathrm{III}}$ and $\mathrm{Eu}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ions as structural probes, providing highly specific local information on the chiral environment of the lanthanide metal ion.66,67 Due to the lability of the $\mathrm{Ln}^{\mathrm{III}}$ centre and because there is no preferential coordination geometry on such complexes, the synthesis of pure enantiomeric complexes is particularly difficult. Common types of chiral ligands that have been used to prepare $\mathrm{Ln}^{\mathrm{III}}$ complexes with predetermined chirality are bipyridine ligands substituted with pinene units, terpyridine ligands, 1,4,7,10-tetraazacyclododecane (cyclen) derivatives and multidentate ligands (Fig. 4). Recently, the synthesis of enantiopure $\mathrm{Ln}^{\mathrm{III}}$ complexes from a chiral bipyridine carboxylate ligand L8 was described by Mamula and co-workers (Fig. 4). These complexes display different helical arrangements of the ligands around the metal centre and appear ‘‘solvent-adaptative’’, that is their structure was tunable upon using different solvents.68,69
Raymond and co-workers have described a panel of Tb and Eu complexes of enantiopure ligands showing strong luminescence and CPL activity in aqueous solution.70,71 The octadentate chiral ligand L9 with four 2-hydroxyisophthalamide arms acts as an efficient antenna when complexed to lanthanide ions such as ${\mathrm{Tb}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}^{\mathrm{III}}$ , DyIII or $\mathrm{Sm}^{\mathrm{III}}$ .70 These thermodynamically stable complexes display sharp well-defined luminescent excitations at different wavelengths, depending on the lanthanide ion. Moreover, the large number of donor atoms in L9 protects the metal centre from any luminescence deactivation through non-radiative interaction with the environment. In these complexes, there is no clear relationship between the chirality of the asymmetric centres in the ligand and the stereochemistry around the metal centre. The absolute configuration assignment is therefore a difficult task, as no crystallographic data are available for these complexes. In the case of $C_{4}$ -symmetric tetraamide ligand L10, the local stereochemistry around the metal ion was studied by NIR-CD and was derived by comparison with known compounds of the same type.72,73 These examples illustrate well that when the chirality of the ligand is remote from the metal centre, it becomes more difficult to generate a stereochemically rigid and well-defined complex, and the absolute configuration assignment is not straightforward. Nevertheless, if the metal centre displays a unique property, e.g. polarized luminescence, the complexes are still of great interest for applications such as biological imaging. There are increasing examples of chiral luminescent lanthanide coordination complexes in the literature, such as recently prepared chiral bundles with controlled stereochemistry from the ligand, where $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interactions play an important role in stabilizing the bundles, and in the generation of self-assembled 3D structures.74,75 Using very simple chiral ligands and lanthanide ions, chiral nanoballs were obtained where the array of lanthanide ions are arranged as in the ferritin biological molecule. Moreover these nanoballs appeared to self-assemble into homochiral helices.75 This example illustrates well the fact that with very simple chiral ligands used as synthons in coordination chemistry, sophisticated chiral assemblies which mimic biological systems can be obtained.
2.3.2 Chiroptical switches. Chirality is important in molecular motors and switches.34 A molecular switch is a molecule than can reversibly interconvert between two stable states upon an external stimulus. Canary and co-workers have used redox chemistry to trigger a chiroptical response in propeller-shaped coordination complexes.76–78 In complexes 8a,b (Scheme 7), the $\mathrm{{Cu}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ centre is coordinated to a chiral ligand bearing a methionine moiety which associates via the carboxylate group. Upon reduction to $\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{I}}$ , the carboxylate dissociates in favour of the softer sulfur group. Because such complexes display strong exciton coupling CD (ECCD),79 they correspond to two distinct states which can be reversibly switched by oxido-reduction. Another example of redox switched ECCD utilizes the different coordination number of the $\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{I}}$ and the $\mathrm{{Cu}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ ions, causing one arm of a tripodal ligand to dissociate upon reduction. Because the tripodal ligand is chiral, the ECCD response is different for the two different states.80,81
In 2001, Branda and co-workers described a chiroptical switch based on photochromes that exhibit two different states with significantly different optical rotations.82 They had the idea to use coordination chemistry to achieve photochromic reactions where only one stereoisomer was formed as described in Scheme 8. 1,2-Dithienylethenes derivatized with chiral oxazoline moieties were stereoselectively coordinated to $\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{I}}$ and produced helicate $\mathbf{9a}$ (see below) whose handedness was dictated by the 1,2-dithienylethene backbone. The thiophene rings in 9a were therefore pre-oriented, such that ring closure gave only one single diastereomer 9b. In this manner, the optical rotation could be reversibly switched in a non-destructive readout process.
## 3. Chiral transfer in supramolecular assemblies and molecular materials
Supramolecular chirality is expressed in assemblies through supramolecular interactions such as hydrogen bonding, van der Waals interactions and metal–ligand coordinative interactions. Chiral assemblies generally result from equilibria allowing the formation of thermodynamically stable ensembles via supramolecular interactions. A great diversity of supramolecular architectures based on coordination compounds are known, among which are helicates, trigonal antiprisms, tetrahedra, octahedra, polygons, etc.83–86 Chiral liquid crystals are another class of supramolecular assemblies that may be generated by coordination of pro-mesogenic ligands with a metal template. Furthermore, thanks to the great diversity, stability and the directionality of metal–ligand interactions, many different chiral coordination polymers may be accessed, as well as polyoxometallates. In relation with the helicity of the bulk material, a large panel of properties and applications arise from these different assemblies.
## 3.1 Helicates
The term helicate was introduced by Lehn in 1987 for the description of a polymetallic helical double-stranded complex.87 The importance of helicates is related to the understanding of self-processes in supramolecular chemistry (molecular recognition, self-organization, self-assembly, supramolecular programming). A significant amount of work has been conducted on helicates and this topic is welldocumented.88–93 In helicates, chirality can be transferred between the metal ions of the molecular entity, and it can also be extended through the formation of high-dimensional chiral polymers. At the molecular level, according to Duan and co-workers, ‘‘the chirality translation between the metal centres only occurs when the bridging modes of the ligands exhibit atropisomeric chiralities in the formation of a helicate.’’94 This means that the helicate’s chirality is determined by the ligand itself (commonly called a helicand) rather than by the metal centres. Helicands are usually composed of several bridging moieties linked by spacers. Note that an efficient way of preparing enantiopure helicates consists of using a chiral unity used as a template. It connects and preorganizes the ligands that will produce the helical strands with a predetermined helicity around the metals. Selected chiral templates are biphenyl derivatives, DNA or hydrogen bonding chiral diols.95–97
In order to have efficient chiral transfer throughout the supramolecular assembly, additional functional groups attached to the ligand can favour the extension of homochirality through intermolecular weak interactions (H-bonding, $\mathrm{CH}{-}\pi$ interactions, etc). Moreover, when chiral assemblies are formed from achiral ligands and metal centres, the transfer of chirality is effective if a spontaneous resolution process occurs during the crystallisation.4,98,99 Indeed, only in these cases the crystals are conglomerates consisting of only $P$ or $M$ helicity, as opposed to racemates where $P$ and $M$ helices coexist, resulting in an overall centrosymmetric crystal.4 For example, spontaneous resolution of silver helicates $\mathrm{[Ag_{2}(L11)_{2}]}^{2+}$ and $\begin{array}{r}{[\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}_{2}(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{1}\pmb{2})_{2}]^{2+}}\end{array}$ has been achieved from achiral ligands $N^{\prime},N^{\prime}$ -bis[1-(imidazol-4-yl)methylidene]benzil dihydrazone L11 and $N^{\prime},N^{\prime}$ -bis[1-(pyridin-2-yl)methylidene]- benzil dihydrazone L12, respectively (Fig. 5). The atropisomeric chiral bridging mode of the ligands translated the chirality from the first metal centre to the second one, resulting in the formation of the chiral molecular helicate. Furthermore, the intermolecular $\mathrm{C-H}\cdot\cdot\pi$ and/or $\pi-\pi$ stacking interactions were strong enough to control the chiral aggregation.100 Finally, amplification of chirality by symmetry breaking was observed during the crystallization process.
Yan and co-workers have described in 2004 an elegant example of chiral transfer in coordination polymers where achiral diazine ligands such as L13–15 (Fig. 6) bearing two bidentate pyridylimino groups were locked upon binding into a twisted chiral conformation, giving rise to chiral bi- or trinuclear helicates.101 In the case of L13, the crystallisation process occurred with spontaneous resolution where the chirality of the molecular units was transferred into homochiral long 1D chains by azido groups interconnecting two different units. Complete chiral transfer was observed in this $\begin{array}{r}{[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}(\mathbf L\mathbf{1}3)_{2}(\mathbf N_{3})_{6}]_{n}}\end{array}$ structure because the long 1D chains were interacting via hydrogen bonding in a homochiral fashion. In the case of L14, no chiral transfer was observed because the molecular units of the $[\bf M n_{2}(\bf L14)(\bf N_{3})_{4}]_{n}$ structure were interconnected into homochiral 2D layers, interacting each other in a heterochiral fashion, therefore giving rise to centrosymmetric units. In the case of L15, partial chiral transfer occurred because the homochiral 2D layers were interacting simultaneously in hetero- and homo-chiral fashion, yielding a mixture of conglomerate and racemate. It was shown in this example that the azido group played an important role in the formation of homochiral 1D polymeric chains and 2D layers because it acted as an intermolecular bridging ligand. However, a minute structural variation at the molecular level could induce dramatic changes in chirality transmission and in the supramolecular structure, yielding different materials displaying very different interesting properties (ferri- and ferromagnetic behaviour) but difficult to predict and rationalize.
## 3.2 Chiral sensing in porphyrin tweezers
Chiral transfer phenomena can be used for sensing chirality of a wide range of chiral molecules, as well as for developing novel chiroptical devices and chiral materials.102 For example, porphyrin tweezer 10 in Scheme 9 is a bis(zinc porphyrin) dimer used as a flexible and achiral receptor. Upon binding of a specific chiral guest such as a chiral diamine, aminoacid or aminoalcohol, the chirality of the guest transfers to the receptor, resulting in a porphyrin helicity. The large induced $\mathrm{ECCD}^{103-108}$ can be used to detect the chirality of the guest (see Scheme 9). Porphyrin tweezers are therefore powerful tools for chiral discrimination studies.
Metal porphyrins are involved in many multifunctional chiral systems involving chiral sensing,104,109,110 chiral memory,111 positive and negative allosteric effects.112 A classical example consists of cerium(IV) bis(porphyrinate) double-decker 11 (Scheme 10), which is in a rapid equilibrium between the right- and left-handed conformations. The 4-pyridyl groups act as hydrogen-bond acceptors and upon binding of a chiral diacid, the equilibrium is shifted to one conformer, resulting in an induced CD in the region of the host chromophore. This is an illustration of cooperative effects of the four pairs of pyridyls groups. Moreover, chiral memory effects are observed upon removal of the chiral guest.113 Note that cooperative effects are often encountered in biological systems and are also important in chiral systems such as chiral sensors, catalysts and actuators.110,113
## 3.3 Metallasupramolecular chiral boxes
Metallasupramolecular chiral boxes are interesting objects because they are self-assembled polyhedra which produce confined chiral environments in which one can imagine to conduct an enantioselective catalysis,114 or recognition of chiral guests. There are different ways of generating chiral metallasupramolecular boxes.115 Raymond and collaborators have described a $\left[\mathrm{Ga}_{4}({\bf L16})_{6}\right]^{12-}$ tetrahedron (Scheme 11) produced as a racemic mixture of homochiral (LLLL and DDDD) structures that could be resolved into enantiomeric clusters using a chiral encapsulated counteraction, methylnicotinium.116 Remarkably, after changing the resolving agent for the achiral encapsulated countercation $\mathrm{Et}_{4}\mathrm{N}^{+}$ , the aqueous solution of the DDDD- $\scriptstyle\left[\mathrm{Et}_{4}\mathrm{N}\subset\mathrm{Ga}_{4}(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{16})_{6}\right]^{11-}$ cluster retained its enantiopurity for months, as evidenced by its persistent CD spectrum. Furthermore, when ligands L16 were stepwise replaced by ligands L17 which are known to normally produce helicate structures $[\mathrm{Ga}_{2}(\mathbf{L17})_{3}]^{6-}$ , not only the chirality was maintained but also the tetrahedral structure remained as a $\Delta\Delta\Delta\mathsf{-}[\mathrm{Et}_{4}\mathbf{N}{\subset}\mathrm{Ga}_{4}(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{1}6)_{6-n}(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{1}7)_{n}]^{11-}$ cluster. In this example, the chirality, which is a property of the structure itself and not of the constituent components, acts as a structural reporter.
## 3.4 Chiral transfer in metallomesogens
Chirality plays an integral role in the molecular organization of many liquid crystals and, consequently, in the design of liquid crystal-based supramolecular materials. For example, the appearance of helical organization in liquid crystals gives rise to fast switching ferro- and antiferroelectric liquid crystals functional materials used for displays,117 second-order non linear optical properties,118 selective reflection119 or electrooptic switching.120 Metal-containing liquid crystals commonly called metallomesogens, are usually built by complexation of ligands with pre-mesogenic character. When stereogenic elements exist in the molecular complexes, helical supramolecular architectures can be obtained. Three different complexes resulting in three different types of helical superstructures are depicted in Scheme 12. The origin of the chiral transfer from the molecular level to the supramolecular organization is different in each case.121 In the case of complex 12 (a),122,123 the introduction of stereogenic centres into the peripheral tails of planar disk-like $\upbeta$ -diketonates led to a rectangular columnar mesophase with helical arrangement within each column. This mesophase displayed an important ECCD coming from the mutual arrangement of the $\upbeta$ -diketone groups through the helical columns. In the case of complex 13 (b),124 hexagonal columnar mesophases with interdigitated stacking of octahedral complexes were obtained, where the chiral tails force the fluxional $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ metal centre to adopt the same $\Lambda$ or $\Delta$ configuration within the helical columns. The predominance of only one configurational isomer is strongly favoured by the fluxionality around the metal centre. Therefore, a strong CD response was measured in the mesophase of the FeIII complexes. In contrast, the mesophase obtained from CoIII displayed much smaller optical activity. This was explained by a locked configuration around the metal centre with impossible interconversion and therefore no cooperative effect within the columnar mesophase. In the case of complex 14 (c),125 the coordination to $\mathrm{Pd}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ metallic ions of chiral oxazoline ligands substituted with pro-mesogenic groups gave polycatenar metallomesogens with helical columnar organization. The complexes displayed $C_{2}$ -symmetry where the two methyl groups at the stereogenic centres were lying on the same side of the molecular plane. A structural model was proposed to account for the chiral transfer from the stereogenic centres of the rigid core to the supramolecular organization, in which an assembly of helical columns adopts a 2D packing. The optimized molecular packing in such structures was shown to be of great importance. Interestingly, in the non-optically active mesophase (comprised of a $50:50$ mixture of both enantiomers of chiral complexes), the same type of helical organization was observed and the authors proposed the existence of segregated domains, either corresponding to columns with the same configurational isomer, or to segregated domains of each configurational isomer along the column axis.
Selected other examples of metallomesogens with helical organization are $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{\mathrm{I}}$ complexes with bipyridine ligands substituted with pendant tails derived from citronellol. These complexes display hexagonal columnar phases only when the counteranion is coordinating $(\mathrm{TfO^{-}})$ because the latter ensures the connection between each molecular building block of the helical columns, through $\operatorname{Ag-O}$ interactions.126
3.5 Metal–organic frameworks Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are crystalline compounds consisting of metal ions or clusters coordinated to organic molecules to form one-, two- or three-dimensional structures of various porosities. They are attracting attention because of their potential applications in catalysis, photonics, separation, gas storage, molecular sensing, and even drug delivery. Compared to conventionally used microporous inorganic materials such as zeolites,127 these organic–inorganic structures have the potential for more flexible rational design, through control of the architecture and functionalization of the pores. Chiral MOFs are, beyond the more fundamental search for new topologies, highly attractive for asymmetric catalysis, chiral recognition, and applications requiring noncentrosymmetric crystal structures such as nonlinear optical devices.128–132 There exist essentially three approaches for the preparation of chiral MOFs. They can be built from achiral components, which may give chiral crystals under spontaneous resolution.98 The chirality of these MOF originates from the spatial disposition of their building blocks and is not amenable to fine-tuning. Moreover, the bulk materials tend to be racemic even though single crystals are chiral. In rare cases, an asymmetric transformation toward a chiral bulk solid occurs during the crystallization process such as in the homochiral network formed from 5-(9-anthracenyl)- pyrimidine organic linker and $\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .133 A second approach utilizes chiral co-ligands in addition to achiral connectors, such as in the homochiral network formed from metal 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylates of the type $\mathbf{M}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}$ which are known to adopt an intrinsically chiral (10,3)-a network structure and from 1,2-propanediol as a chiral template.134 A third and more straightforward approach consists of combining metal-containing nodes and chiral organic bridging ligands. This is the most often used method because of its flexibility and reliability. The use of chiral bridging ligands will ensure the chirality of the resulting network structures. Various chiral bridging ligands have been utilized to construct chiral MOFs. For example, ‘‘bioinspired’’ MOFs have recently been prepared by using peptides as chiral connectors.135 Chiral oxazoline derivatives are efficient ligands to generate chiral coordination complexes, such as $C_{2}$ -symmetric 2,6-bis(oxazolyl)pyridine, a chiral tecton used to generate a chiral and directional 1D network of the formula $\mathrm{Co}(\mathbf{L18})\mathrm{Cl}_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ (Fig. 7).136 These 1D chains adopt parallel packing in the solid in a non-centrosymmetric arrangement. The molecular chirality is thus successfully used to align molecular dipoles leading to a polar crystal.
The next example depicted in Fig. 8 shows a chiral MOF obtained from a binaphthyl derivative, an important class of ligands for predetermined chirality, especially for the construction of chiral metallocycles used for chiral sensing and asymmetric catalysis.137,138 Indeed, axially chiral bridging ligand $(R)\ –6,6^{\prime}$ -dichloro- $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -dihydroxy- $\cdot1,1^{\prime}$ -binaphthyl- $^{\cdot4,4^{\prime}\cdot}$ - bipyridine, L19 (Fig. 8(a)) which contains the bipyridyl primary functionality and orthogonal chiral $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -dihydroxy secondary functionality, was used to construct homochiral porous MOFs 15 of the formula $\mathbf{\lbrackCd_{3}C l_{6}L19_{3}]}$ 4DMF $6\mathrm{MeOH}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ 139,140 Octahedrally coordinated $\mathrm{Cd}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centres in 15 are doubly bridged by the chlorides to form 1D zigzag $[{\mathrm{Cd}}(\upmu\mathrm{-Cl})_{2}]_{n}$ chains that serve as secondary building units (SBUs). Each $\mathrm{Cd}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centre in the 1D zigzag chain further coordinates to two pyridyl groups of the L19 ligands and connects adjacent 1D $[\mathrm{Cd}(\upmu\mathrm{-Cl})_{2}]_{n}$ SBUs to form a noninterpenetrating 3D network with very large chiral channels of $\sim1.6~\times~1.8~\mathrm{\nm}$ cross-section (Fig. 8(c)). This synthetic strategy led to efficient heterogeneous asymmetric catalysts. Indeed, by taking advantage of the readily accessible chiral dihydroxy groups which can react with ${\mathrm{Ti}}(\mathrm{O}^{i}{\mathrm{Pr}})_{4}$ , an active species 15 Ti (Fig. 8(b)) catalyzed the addition of $\mathrm{ZnEt}_{2}$ to prochiral aldehyde to afford chiral alcohols with very high enantiomeric excesses.
Kim et al. reported the first example of asymmetric catalysis using a crystalline homochiral MOF.141 The strategy used to prepare rigid, homochiral frameworks with large chiral voids exploited large, robust, enantiopure chiral metallorganic clusters as building blocks142 which consisted of oxo-bridged trinuclear metal carboxylates $[\mathrm{M}_{3}(\upmu_{3}{\mathrm{-O}})(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CR})_{6}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]^{n+}$ 1 $\mathbf{M}=$ divalent or trivalent transition metal ions, $\mathbf{O}_{2}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{R}=$ organic carboxylate anions) (Fig. 9a). The use of a rigid chiral organic molecule derived from tartaric acid (Fig. 9(b)) with a carboxylic group and a pyridyl group could lead both to the formation of oxo-bridged trinuclear metal carboxylate units and to the linking of these trimers into two- or three-dimensional frameworks. It therefore gave rise to a chiral MOF $[\mathrm{Zn_{3}(\mu_{3}-O)(L20-H)_{6}}]$ - $2\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}{\cdot}12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (named D-POST-1) (Fig. 9(c)) possessing a layer structure with large chiral 1D channels of $\mathrm{\sim}13.4\mathring\mathrm{A}$ in dimension along the $c$ -axis. Owing to the chiral environment of the channel, enantioselective inclusion of metal complexes $[\mathrm{Ru}(2,2^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bipy})_{3}]\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ could be achieved. Moreover the accessible pyridyl group in the channels gave an opportunity to modulate the characteristics of the chiral MOFs such as the size of their pores or their charges. The presence of these pyridyl groups exposed in the channels also provided POST-1 with catalytic properties, as evidenced by the kinetic resolution of rac-1-phenyl-2-propanol via transesterification. Other selected applications in chiral technologies are enantiomeric separations as recently reported in the separation of chiral sulfoxide by using a 3D porous $Z\mathrm{n}$ -organic framework $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(b d c)(L\mathrm{-lac)(dmf)]}\mathrm{\cdotDMF}}$ (where bdc $\mathit{\Pi}=\mathit{\Pi}_{p}$ -benzenedicarboxylic acid, $\mathrm{d}{\mathrm{mf}}\ =\ N,N^{\prime}.$ - dimethylformamide), possessing intrinsic chirality due to the chiral L-lac ( $\dot{\mathbf{\Phi}}=\mathbf{\Phi}_{\mathrm{L}}$ -lactic acid) and displaying both catalytic and enantioselective sorption properties.143,144
## 3.6 Chiral magnets based on coordination polymers
The development of materials displaying long-range magnetic ordering and optical activity is attracting attention in the field of molecular magnetism because such materials are able to display magneto-chiral effects (MCh), which are a combination of Faraday effects and natural circular dichroism.145–147
Chiral and magnetic properties must be controlled both at the molecular and at the supramolecular level. Chirality may arise from spontaneous resolution but such cases are rare and difficult to control. As described throughout this review, a general method to prepare enantioenriched coordination compounds involves the transfer of chirality from an enantioenriched ligand to a metal ion, accompanied by a control of its configuration.148–150 Another strategy is to use resolved trischelated metal complexes151–155 or chiral ions as chiral templates in the formation of supramolecular structures (chiral inductive effect).141 In this field of research, chiral bimetallic oxalate based polymers are an illustrative example, because they can lead to chiral magnetic materials with controlled 2D or 3D dimensionality.151 Indeed, chiral 3D structures of the general formula $[\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{H}}(\mathbf{{bpy}})_{3})][\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{I}}\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathbf{{ox}})_{3}]$ and $[\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{H}}(\mathbf{bpy})_{3})][\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{H}}{}_{2}(\mathbf{ox})_{3}]$ have been described, where the propellerlike mononuclear subunits assemble in a homochiral way (all $\Delta$ or all $\Lambda$ ). These structures correspond to well-defined 3D 3-connected 10-gon nets (10,3) with $[\bar{\bf M}^{\mathrm{II}}(\bar{\bf b}\mathrm{py})_{3})]^{2+}$ cations occupying the vacancies, as for the two homochiral enantiomeric three-dimensional coordination polymers $[\Lambda{\cdot}\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3})].$ $[\Lambda\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]$ L-L-16 and $[\Delta\mathrm{-Ru(bpy)}_{3})][\Delta\mathrm{-Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\ \Delta\mathrm{-}\Delta\mathrm{-}16$ depicted in Fig. 10.154 In such homochiral structures, the anionic network corresponds to three sets of interconnected homochiral helices and also defines cavities of $D_{3}$ -symmetry in which the counter-cations can be incorporated, also in a helicoidal fashion. Bimetallic 2D optically active networks correspond to 6-gon 3-connected anionic networks (6,3) of the general formula $[\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathbf{ox})_{3}][\mathbf{C}]$ (with $\mathrm{C}^{+}$ a countercation) with honeycomb-like layers consisting of alternating metal centers $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}$ or $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{III}}$ , each metal type displaying the same configuration (see for example complex 17 in Scheme 13). As depicted in Fig. 11, the formation of 2D or 3D polymeric networks depends on the heterochirality or homochirality of the bimetallic anionic network. Indeed, heterochiral bimetallic oxalates are present in 2D-honeycomb polymers while homochiral bimetallic oxalates lead to 3D-helical polymers. The control of the structure and chirality of 2D polymers is less obvious. More details about these chiral magnetic materials may be found in ref. 153.
Recently, Train et al. have successfully prepared a 2D bimetallic oxalate bridged network in its two enantiomeric forms by using as a chiral template a tetraalkylammonium countercation with one chiral carbon on one alkyl chain.156,157 This material of general formula $\mathrm{N}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})(n{-}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{7})_{2}(s{-}\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{9}){\cdot}$ - [S-L-Mn- $\Delta{\mathrm{-Cr}}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]$ $S_{\mathrm{N}^{-}}\Lambda_{\mathrm{Mn}^{-}}\Delta_{\mathrm{Cr}^{-17}}$ revealed very interesting magnetic properties, with an abrupt paramagnetic-to-ferromagnetic phase transition at $7\mathrm{~K~}$ . Strong magnetochiral effects145–147 (Scheme 13) could then be measured for the first time on a ferromagnet and MCh values were 17 times greater in the ferromagnetic phase than in the paramagnetic one. This nice result allows the possibility to use magnetochirality for data storage and opens the way to new chiral multifunctional materials.
## 3.7 Chiral transfer in polyoxometallates
The term polyoxometallate (abbreviated POM) covers an extremely large group of generally anionic clusters with frameworks built from transition metal oxo anions linked by shared oxide ions. Stable enantiopure nanosized polyoxoanions can be useful chiral materials in the fields of microporous solids, inorganic pharmaceuticals or catalysts for homogeneous asymmetric oxidation. Chirality has rarely been explored in POM systems.158 Realization of an intrinsically chiral and configurationally stable POM is a difficult issue because usually these chiral systems undergo rapid racemization in solution. However, in 2005, Hill et al. have demonstrated the amplification and transfer of chirality from small organic tartrate molecules to much larger multinanometer polyoxotungstates through high-coordinate zirconium centres.159 Indeed they synthesized enantiomerically pure polyoxotungstate having the following formula: $\{[{\alpha}\mathrm{-P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{15}\mathrm{O}_{55}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]Z\mathrm{r}_{3}(\mathrm{\textmu}_{3}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{O})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})(\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{tart}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H})[{\alpha}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{16}\mathrm{O}_{59}]\}^{15-}$ (18, Fig. 12). During the formation process, the tartrate molecules produce a chiral environment which is transferred to the achiral POM through zirconium(IV) ions that strongly bind to the tartrates and to the POM. In solution, an induced circular dichroism (ICD) was detected. The $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray structure of 18 showed how the asymmetry of the tartrate was extended to the POM by the zirconium ions, giving an inherently chiral species. This chirality was persistent in solution as evidenced by CD spectroscopy. Another approach to chiral POMs with persistent optical activity in solution consists of preparing chiral polyoxoanions and linking these discrete enantiomerically pure POMs by simple metal ions. This has been recently achieved by Sun, Wang et al.,160,161 by manually resolving the enantiomorph crystals of $\mathrm{Zn(H_{2}O)_{3})_{3}[M n M o_{9}O_{32}]{\cdot}4H_{2}O}$ 19 enantiomers. The $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray structure of the enantiomorph crystals revealed a 3D chiral framework assembled from $\mathrm{[MnMo_{9}O_{32}]^{6-}}$ clusters and $\left(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}\right.^{2+}$ groups (see Fig. 12). Recently, chiral Strandberg-type molybdates $[(\mathrm{RPO}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{MO}_{5}\mathrm{O}_{15}]^{2-}$ were prepared, with $\mathtt{R}$ being a chiral hydrogen-bonding chain such as $\mathrm{-CH_{3}C H(N H_{3})}$ , and displayed self-assembled fibrillar nanostructures with enhanced optical activity.162
## 4. Conclusion
Several chiral functional materials have been prepared up to now, in which the chiral transfer is effective. In some cases, the chiral transfer is controlled and well-understood. However, it is still difficult to extract some general rules that govern chiral transfer at the molecular level and in materials. The helical structure of a material should be encoded in the molecular building block itself and should affect molecular characteristics such as shape, topology, and/or stereochemistry. The design of the molecular building block is therefore a crucial issue. Helicity is one important target in supramolecular chemistry and material science for several reasons. First, it enables to gain understanding in the formation of complex supramolecular architectures by processes such as self-assembly or self-organization. Second, the chirality of molecular building blocks is often transferred to the bulk material thus providing original chiral superstructures. Third, a chiral helical organization may produce a functional material with specific properties such as chiroptical properties, or non linear optical properties due to the non-centrosymmetric orientation of NLOphores in helical suprastructures.
